From: Folio
Subject: [PATCH] calc(), custom properties with var(), and display: grid

    Three things RCSS lacks that a stylesheet written by someone who knows CSS reaches for first.

    calc(): a sum of lengths and one percentage, parsed into a coefficient per unit and interned. The
    lengths resolve when values are computed and the percentage at layout, through a Calc type on
    LengthPercentage(Auto) whose value indexes the resolved pair. A sum of one unit is an ordinary value,
    so calc(2 * 8dp) still animates.

    var(): `--name: value` is kept per declaration block as written and inherited by lookup up the tree.
    A declaration using var() is held unparsed, with the shorthand it came from, and parsed against the
    element when its values are computed. A change to an element's custom properties dirties every
    property below it that uses var(). Rules holding only custom properties are indexed like any other.

    display: grid: grid-template-columns and -rows (lengths, percentages, fr, auto, minmax, repeat),
    grid-auto-columns and -rows, grid-auto-flow, grid-column and grid-row (lines, negative lines, span),
    justify-items and justify-self, and the existing gap and align-items. Sparse auto-placement; no named
    lines or areas, dense packing or subgrid. fr has no content minimum, as minmax(0, 1fr). align-items
    and align-self also accept start and end.

    Removable once upstream has all three.
---
